## 6 · Rollback and recovery map

| Scenario | Mechanism (v4) | Retention / budget | Read-back | v3 state (FACT) |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| **Bad release** | `hee4 release rollback` (V4-15, *rev 2026-10-01*): move `current` to the previous release dir by atomic rename (the same door as install, IR:211-216), then `systemctl --user restart hee4.service` and D2 read-back | **Release retention (PROPOSAL):** keep `current`, the previous release, and every release named by a host record at an ancestor of main. Prune the rest (`hee4 release prune`, V4-15) through a pure policy over the directory listing, which prints `keep=K prune=P bytes=B`; `--apply` needs a dry-run file of the same digest. Never prune `current` or the rollback target. | `readlink -f current` before and after; `/proc/<MainPID>/exe` digest = the target manifest | no rollback verb; 9 releases, 264 MB, pruned by nothing (M-1001; RT §3.6) |
| **Engine state lost or corrupt** | `serve` backups (manifest-last, backup/2) to `/var/mnt/STORAGE-10TB/hee4-backups/<id>/` on a separate physical disk *(rev 2026-10-01, V4-11)*. `hee4 restore --into` a fresh generation (V4-15). `recovery` reconciles to `complete` before any dispatch (CD:73: RTO ≤10 min, RPO ≤35 min) | **128 GiB backup budget** (CD:67), enforced fail-closed at admission, with 256 GiB headroom (DR:1804). v4: `prune-backups` is a pure policy (keep the newest verified backup and each daily one for N days; PROPOSAL N=7) that **never** removes the only verified backup and prints both numbers. Accepted and open-obligation evidence is never auto-deleted (CD:67) | restore line with `rto_s` and object parity; D5 | budget enforced; prune is "by hand" (DR:1804); no restore verb (MA row 4) |
| **Roster / observation growth** | Ledgered retention: the latest observation per record, plus rows pinned by an attempt; a per-principal cap | Pure policy with a failing-first test (DR:1895) | `roster.list` count ≤ cap after N synthetic attempts in the gate | global 4096 cap, never pruned: a suspected blocker after about 4096 attempts (DR:1892-1894) |
| **Host reboot or logout mid-attempt** | The unit is login-only. SIGTERM → drain waits for the in-flight attempt under its own deadline (dispatcher drain, DR:816). `TimeoutStopSec` = 1,200 s + the measured seal time. On restart, recovery reconciles `effect_unknown` | n/a | after restart, `health recovery=complete`, and `task.get` shows the reconciled terminal | t3a was interrupted by a KDE session reboot with a staged ostree deployment (FC2:118-119); the ledger survived for the post-mortem |
| **Migration / upgrade** | an upgrade verb that takes a backup first (HO-08); one owner per migration number | pre-upgrade backup retained until the next verified backup | `PRAGMA user_version`, migration_history rows | tripwire `CURRENT == 8`, upgrade verb unbuilt (DR:1384) |
| **Model daemon absent or changed** | HP-rows (host preconditions, *rev 2026-10-01, V2 X4*) refuse before dispatch: holder exe digest ≠ pin, or `/api/ps` identity ≠ profile → `unavailable` by name. No silent CPU fallback: the `size_vram > 0` row | n/a | HP8-style row | the endpoint rule landed (N6b, DR:1859) |
| **Codebase / evidence loss** | the `hee4-backup` habitat unit (the `hee3-backup` pattern: `rsync --link-dest` to the other disk + a drill `verdict=PASS compared=N`) | snapshot retention as hee3-backup's (UNMEASURED(P7): hee3-backup's own prune rule was not read for this atlas) | `journalctl --user -u hee4-backup` verdict lines (Luke's own unit logs; H-7 applies to engine data) | `hee3-backup.timer` enabled, next run 2026-10-02 01:36 (M-1001) |
| **Whole-machine loss** | none. There is no off-site copy (CL durability) | — | — | out of scope (CD:71: "does not promise survival of whole-device loss"); H-15 |

